14:56:33 [[chatgpt-bridge]] chatgpt-bridge.mcp INFO Tool call: name=ask_chatgpt args={'prompt': 'I\'m building an electrical engineer portfolio in Next.js 16.3.5 / React 19.2.8 / TypeScript 5.9.3 with App Router, no Tailwind, custom CSS, and src/ directory. Import alias: @/* → ./src/*\n\n## What\'s been done\n- Next.js scaffold with TypeScript, ESLint, no Tailwind\n- Global design system in `src/app/globals.css`: color tokens (Carbon Black #080A0A base, Signal Green #B6FF4A, Logic Cyan #6EDCFF, Power Amber #FFB547, Fault Red #FF604A), Bricolage Grotesque (display) + IBM Plex Mono (monospace) via next/font/google\n- Container component at `src/components/layout/Container.tsx`\n- Sticky Header at `src/components/layout/Header.tsx` (server component) + MobileNavigation at `src/components/layout/MobileNavigation.tsx` (client component)\n- Route skeletons: /, /projects, /about, /contact\n\n## What\'s next: Step 4 — Framer Motion configuration + reduced-motion infrastructure\n\nThe plan.md specifies:\n- Required motion primitives: useScroll, useTransform, useMotionValue, useSpring, useInView, AnimatePresence, layoutId, motion.path, MotionConfig\n- Must respect `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` — remove scroll-linked pulses, parallax, cursor inertia; replace complex sequences with simple fades; retain all information\n- Site must remain completely usable without animation\n\n## What I need you to tell me\n\nBuild out the Framer Motion infrastructure. Specifically:\n\n1. **Install framer-motion** (latest compatible with React 19)\n\n2. **`src/hooks/useReducedMotionSafe.ts`** — A custom hook that returns `true` if the user prefers reduced motion OR is on a touch device. Should use `window.matchMedia(\'(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\')` and check `window.matchMedia(\'(pointer: coarse)\')`. Should be a client component, use useState + useEffect for SSR safety (initial value from `typeof window !== \'undefined\'`, then subscribe to media query changes).\n\n3. **`src/components/motion/MotionProvider.tsx`** — A client component that wraps the app\'s motion configuration. Uses `<MotionConfig>` from framer-motion. Should:\n   - Read the reduced-motion preference\n   - When reduced motion is preferred, set `transition={{ duration: 0, ease: "linear" }}` as default to cancel all animations\n   - When not reduced motion, use smooth spring defaults: `transition={{ type: "spring", damping: 25, stiffness: 200 }}`\n   - Provide the reduced-motion state via React context so child components can adapt\n\n4. **`src/hooks/usePointerPosition.ts`** — A client hook that tracks mouse/touch position globally. Returns `{ x: number, y: number }`. Uses `useMotionValue` from framer-motion and `useMotionValueEvent`. Should be SSR-safe.\n\n5. **`src/hooks/useScrollProgress.ts`** — A client hook that returns scroll progress (0 to 1) for the page. Uses `useScroll` from framer-motion and `useTransform` to map scroll to 0-1 range. SSR-safe.\n\n6. **`src/hooks/useSectionProgress.ts`** — A client hook that takes a `ref` (RefObject<HTMLDivElement | null>) and returns scroll progress (0 to 1) for that specific section. Uses `useScroll` with `target` prop and `offset` for section-specific tracking.\n\n7. **`src/hooks/useDeviceCapability.ts`** — A client hook that returns capability info: `{ isTouch: boolean, isReducedMotion: boolean, pointerType: \'mouse\' | \'touch\' | \'none\' }`. Useful for adaptive interactions.\n\n## Constraints\n- No clsx, classnames, CVA, or tailwind-merge — plain string concatenation or template literals for className joining\n- No Tailwind anywhere\n- SSR-safe: all hooks must handle `typeof window !== \'undefined\'` guard\n- No unnecessary abstraction — keep hooks focused and simple\n- Follow the existing code style: minimal comments, direct naming\n- Use `ComponentPropsWithoutRef` from react, not `forwardRef` unless necessary\n\nPlease provide the exact file contents for each file, with full implementation.', 'timeout': 300000}
