21:17:08 [[chatgpt-bridge]] chatgpt-bridge.mcp INFO Tool call: name=ask_chatgpt args={'prompt': 'I\'m building a GitHub Actions workflow to auto-deploy a Node.js app to a remote Linux server via SSH. Here\'s my plan:\n\n**Workflow file** (.github/workflows/deploy.yml):\n- Triggers on `push` to `master`\n- Runs on `ubuntu-latest`\n- Uses 4 GitHub repository secrets: SSH_HOST, SSH_USER, SSH_PORT, SSH_PRIVATE_KEY\n- The job:\n  1. Creates `~/.ssh/deploy_key` from the secret\n  2. Runs `ssh-keyscan` to populate known_hosts\n  3. Uses `ssh -i` to connect to the remote server\n  4. On the remote: `cd ~/tv-app && git pull origin master && npm install --production && pkill -f "node server.js" && sleep 1 && nohup node server.js & disown`\n  5. Prints the server log\n\n**Secrets to add**:\n- SSH_HOST = `192.168.0.231`\n- SSH_USER = `server`\n- SSH_PORT = `22`\n- SSH_PRIVATE_KEY = full contents of `~/.ssh/githubssh` (an ed25519 key)\n\n**Questions**:\n1. Will this workflow actually work as written? Are there any syntax or logic issues?\n2. Is using `nohup ... & disown` via SSH reliable for keeping the process alive after the SSH session ends?\n3. Is there a better way to keep the Node process running (e.g., systemd service)?\n4. Any security concerns with this approach?\n5. Will `git pull` work without additional auth setup since it\'s using HTTPS (not SSH) for the GitHub clone?', 'timeout': 180000}
