20:24:14 [[chatgpt-bridge]] chatgpt-bridge.mcp INFO Tool call: name=ask_chatgpt args={'prompt': "I'm implementing M4 (monitoring/metrics) for a compact FastAPI homelab dashboard. The project is small — roughly 15 Python files total. Please advise on two design decisions:\n\n## 1. Package structure for monitoring\n\nThe monitoring functionality needs:\n- System metrics collectors (CPU, RAM, uptime, disk, network, temperature)\n- Service health modeling (Samba, TV systemd states)\n- Bounded recent journal logs for fixed allowed units\n- A consolidated GET /api/status endpoint\n\nOptions:\nA) Single `app/monitoring.py` module — all collectors, service health, logs, and status endpoint in one file (~400-600 lines)\nB) `app/monitoring.py` (main module with status endpoint) + `app/monitoring_services.py` (service health) + `app/monitoring_logs.py` (logs) — split by concern\nC) `app/monitoring/` subpackage with `__init__.py`, `collectors.py`, `services.py`, `logs.py`\n\nWhich is best for a project this size? What are the trade-offs?\n\n## 2. Probe timeout strategy for GET /api/status\n\nThe status endpoint aggregates multiple independent probes. Each probe has different characteristics:\n- CPU/RAM: fast psutil calls (~1ms)\n- Disk: filesystem stat calls (~1ms)\n- Network: reading /proc/net/dev or psutil (~1ms)\n- Temperature: reading psutil.sensors_temperatures (~1ms)\n- Service health: subprocess/systemctl calls (~50-200ms)\n- Logs: journalctl subprocess (~50-500ms)\n\nOptions:\nA) Per-probe timeout with independent degradation — each probe has its own timeout (e.g., 2s for subprocess probes, 500ms for fast probes). If one times out, only that field is marked UNKNOWN/stale. The rest of the response still returns.\nB) Global status timeout — one timeout for the entire endpoint (e.g., 5s). If any probe times out, the whole request fails with 504.\nC) Hybrid — fast probes have no timeout, subprocess probes have individual timeouts, and there's a soft global cap.\n\nWhich is best for a dashboard where availability matters more than perfect data? What timeout values would you recommend?\n\nPlease give a clear recommendation for both decisions.", 'timeout': 1800000}
